    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Poly3D can be used to compute the stress, strain and displacement fields in a half or whole space with shearing-mode fractures (i.e., faults). It can also be used to solve problems with opening- mode fractures (i.e., joints, veins and dikes). Whether a fracture is modeled as opening-mode or shearing-mode depends on the local boundary conditions (tractions and displacements) specified on the fracture: When modeling faults, the displacement discontinuities across the fractures in their normal directions are zero; When modeling joints, the normal and tangential tractions on the fracture surfaces are zero; and when modeling veins or dikes, only the normal tractions are non-zero. One application of Poly3D in modeling opening-mode fractures is shown in studying the formation and spacing of orthogonal cross joints presented in this volume (Bai, 2000). In this note, we show a Poly3D verification of the stress state transition between equally-spaced opening-mode fractures in layered materials, an interesting result we found earlier (Bai and Pollard, 2000; Bai et al., 2000) using FRANC2D (Wawrzynek and Ingraffea, 1987).",
